  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nIf you quickly looked at the 500mb analysis between yesterday and \nthis morning, it might seem like little has changed. That is \nsomewhat accurate across the central CONUS and eastern Rockies. \nThe axis of the ridge that dominates the center part of the \ncountry has shifted a little south and east as the trough across\nthe western CONUS moves a bit east northeastward. These subtle \nmovements are the beginning of an overall pattern shift that is \nexpected next week.\n\nFor the next few days, above normal temperatures and dry conditions \ncontinue. Highs will be in the mid to upper 70s with a few spots \ntouching 80 degrees. Winds will probably be the most interesting \nweather variable of the day as diurnal mixing will mix swifter \nflowing 850 flow down to the surface; however, the peak of the 850mb \nflow resides over central KS extending into eastern NE and western \nIA; therefore, gusts (around 20-25 MPH) will likely be concentrated \nto areas along and west of the I-35 to I-49 corridors.\n\nAs Friday becomes Saturday, a trough continues to progress eastward \nacross the western CONUS. Simultaneously a low to midlevel high \nacross the eastern seaboard expands westward toward the MO/KS \nborder. The end result is a cutoff of midlevel flow yielding a warm \nand dry weekend with less gusty winds.\n\nDeterministic model guidance suggests the southern part of the \nwestern trough developing into a cut off low over the four corners \nregion Sunday. This could further the ongoing storms across that \nregion and play a significant role in the weather picture for later \nnext week. There is some skepticism as its imminent formation is not \nfully clear on water vapor satellite imagery, but it is more likely \nto manifest itself towards sunrise Saturday. The primary influence \nthis system would have is enabling development of showers and storms \nalong a synoptic front anticipated to develop from west Texas \nthrough the northern Great Lakes. This front will be competing \nagainst the large high across the eastern CONUS mentioned earlier. \nPrevious model iterations were more optimistic about precipitation \nmaking its was eastward late Monday into Tuesday; however, recent \nruns have been trending drier with precipitation chances around 10-\n25 percent and those chances only making it to the St Joseph area \nand points northwest. Dry and stable air ahead of the front look to \nwin out lowering the chances of rain across the region. However, \nthis does not mean the colder air would be held off. Another upper \nlevel wave pushes a lower level high pressure from interior Canada \ninto the central CONUS which will deliver the cooler air. High \ntemperatures decrease to the lower 70s before rebounding again \nthanks to some sinusoidal action in the 500mb flow; however, another \ntag team of upper level waves and Canadian air masses look to lower \ntemperatures back down toward the upper 60s.\n\nLooking at the extended forecast, guidance suggest further waves \nmoving across the region with a slow moving synoptic scale cold \nfront expected to drape across the central part of the country toward \nthe end of next week. Many models have been using this as a focus \nfor shower and storm development; however, at this time scale models \nusually broad brush precipitation potential. Therefore, \nuncertainties linger with precipitation probabilities currently \nsitting around 10-20 percent Thursday and 20-40 percent Friday with \nmore fluctuations expected as the forecast updates.",
